r  with adrian  she's most likely retrieving the suit  from the closet and using it to execute  her master plan  after cecilia's final confrontation with  adrian the film  ends rather abruptly even though in this  moment cecilia seems to have found some  measure of closure  we still have endless questions about  what comes next for her  first is cecilia going to jail for any  of the stuff she's done  she may not have actually committed her  sister's murder but she did break out of  a mental hospital  and she did kill both tom and adrian  however we think she's going to be just  fine  numerous people saw an invisible suspect  menacing cecilia in the hospital  which means shooting tom was  legitimately self-defense  plus her plan for eliminating adrian was  rock solid  and if things go bad cecilia also just  inherited five million dollars  so she can probably afford some pretty  good lawyers  one of the reasons that the invisible  man has been so critically well received  is because it succeeds on numerous  different levels simultaneously  it's not just a vehicle for scares it's  also a film with a brain  full of deeper themes about trauma and  abuse but what exactly  is it trying to say about these subjects  first  in regards to trauma the film shows us  that even when the source of your pain  is gone  trauma stays with you like an invisible  monster  we see that this is the case for cecilia  even before the invisible man comes back  into her new life  she's afraid to leave the house she has  trouble sleeping and she jumps at  shadows  metaphorically cecelia's tormentor is a  personification of trauma  an invisible force that stands at the  foot of her bed at night  follows her down the street and pulls  her away from her friends and family  and second in regards to abuse the  invisible man shows us that when people  come forward and claim that they're  being abused we tend not to believe them  we downplay their stories invalidate  their feelings  and call them crazy